-  "description": "inventory.native: One-call inventory of a native binary: per architecture slice, its kind, interpreter, own name (soname, DLL name or install name), linked libraries, exported and imported symbols, and language markers. inventory.overview: One call for a first look at a native binary: per slice its architecture, kind, entry point, links, how many sections, segments, symbols (defined and undefined) and procedures it has, and which sections look compressed or encrypted. inventory.segments: The layout of a native binary, per architecture slice: its segments and sections with virtual address, size, file offset, bytes in the file, permissions, type and flags. inventory.symbols: Every symbol a native binary names (ELF .symtab and .dynsym with versions, PE exports and imports, Mach-O nlist, wasm imports, exports and names): address, size, kind, binding, section and library. Filters by name, kind and defined or undefined; pages with offset and limit. inventory.procedures: The procedures (functions) of a native binary with start address, size and name where known, merged from every table that records them: symbols, ELF .eh_frame, PE exports and .pdata, Mach-O nlist and function starts, wasm function bodies. Finds functions in stripped binaries. Filters by name; pages with offset and limit. inventory.notes: The notes of an ELF file and what the well-known ones say: GNU build ID, ABI tag (OS and minimum kernel), the property note (x86 IBT/SHSTK and ISA level, AArch64 BTI/PAC), the Go build ID and FDO packaging metadata. inventory.resources: The resources of a PE file (type, name, language, size and file offset of each) and the contents agents ask about: version information (file and product version and every StringFileInfo value such as CompanyName and OriginalFilename), string tables and the application manifest. inventory.macho: A Mach-O file's header and every load command with its fields (linked dylibs with versions, rpaths, install name, UUID, platform and minimum OS, SDK, entry point, linkedit tables, encryption), per slice, and its code signature: identifier, team, flags such as adhoc and linker-signed, hash type, entitlements, and every page hash recomputed over the file. inventory.pe: A PE file's exploit mitigations and load configuration: ASLR, high-entropy ASLR, DEP, Control Flow Guard, SafeSEH and forced-integrity bits from the header, and the load configuration directory (security cookie, SEH handler table, CFG check and function table, guard flags, enclave and volatile metadata pointers). inventory.model: The header of a GGUF or safetensors model file, whatever its size: format version, byte order, every metadata key with its value (long arrays such as a vocabulary as a length, the first elements and a sha256), the tensor table (name, shape, element type, offset, size) with a total parameter count and a count by quantisation type, and what is wrong with the file (tensors past its end, misaligned offsets). Filters tensors by name; pages with offset and limit. inventory.go: A Go binary's build information (Go version, main module, every dependency with version, sum and replacement, build settings such as GOOS, GOARCH and CGO_ENABLED) and its functions from the pclntab, which survive stripping. Functions filter by name and page with offset and limit. inventory.rust: A Rust binary's dependencies as cargo-auditable embeds them (.dep-v0 in ELF, PE, every Mach-O slice and wasm): format version, every package with version, source, kind, features and root flag, and the dependency edges as indices and names; and the rustc commit and other Rust marks, so a Rust binary without cargo-auditable is still named Rust. Bounded: 8 MiB inflated, 65536 packages, 1048576 edges (4096 listed per package), 8 MiB of read-only data scanned; past a bound or on damage the result is partial. Packages page with offset and limit. Pass operation and its arguments; see session describe for one operation's schema.",
